# Windows: run commands through WSL

When the host OS is Windows (`win32`, PowerShell, or cmd), do not run Shell commands natively. Prefix every command with `wsl` so it executes in the default WSL distro.

## Required pattern

```bash
# ❌ BAD — PowerShell / cmd
git status
npm test
ls src

# ✅ GOOD — WSL first
wsl git status
wsl npm test
wsl ls src
```

For anything with quotes, pipes, `&&`, or a heredoc, wrap the Linux command:

```bash
wsl bash -lc "cd /mnt/c/Users/David/Documents/PROJECTS && git status"
```

## Paths

Convert Windows paths before they reach WSL:

- `C:\Users\David\foo` → `/mnt/c/Users/David/foo`
- Drive letter becomes `/mnt/<letter>/` (lowercase)

If the Shell tool `working_directory` is a Windows path, still prefix the command with `wsl`. Prefer a Linux cwd inside `wsl bash -lc "cd /mnt/c/... && ..."` when the command depends on cwd.

## Do not

- Run `git`, `npm`, `node`, `python`, `ls`, or similar directly in PowerShell
- Use PowerShell syntax (`Get-ChildItem`, `Select-Object`, `$env:FOO`)
- Skip `wsl` because a command "looks simple"
